--TEST--
`Core\Cli::write`'s two options each default to the absence of an effect, and both sides of each are named — ADR 0086 § 3
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `newline` appends exactly one `LF` and an omitted one appends nothing, so the
// member writes precisely the bytes it was handed. Both sides are asserted
// together: a member that always appended is green against a case that only
// asks for the newline, and one that never did is green against its opposite.
Core\Cli::write("a");
Core\Cli::write("b");
Core\Cli::write("c", {newline: true});
Core\Cli::write("d", {newline: false});
echo "\n";

// `stream` defaults to `Out`. `Err` is this request's diagnostic channel rather
// than a second output sink, so nothing written there reaches the bytes below —
// a member that ignored the option would print the second line here.
Core\Cli::write("out", {stream: Core\Cli\Stream::Out, newline: true});
Core\Cli::write("this line is not standard output", {stream: Core\Cli\Stream::Err, newline: true});

// Named in the other order, because `rule:core-api/shape-rules` R2 makes an option callable by its
// own name rather than by its place in the bag.
Core\Cli::write("both", {newline: true, stream: Core\Cli\Stream::Out});
--EXPECT--
abc
d
out
both
